                <pb facs="0147" n="57" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg57"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77 Chinese Eastern/1297: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-02-27T12:00:00+08:00">February 27,
                        1934—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received February 27—2:22 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>101. The following telegram has been received from the Consul General at
                    Harbin.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“February 26, 4 p.m. Engineer Kalina and other five railway prisoners
                        released on February 24th, and expected soon to go to Russia. Their places
                        on railway has been taken by other Soviet Russians. Presumed that Tokyo
                        conference will be resumed and that Soviet Russia will sell railway thereby
                        commencing end of Russian influence in North Manchuria.[”]</p>
                </quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
